Story 1: Centralized Runtime State Definition (state.py)
Epic: Phase 1 - Foundations

Description:

As a developer, I want to define the core centralized data schema so that the orchestration layer and all downstream multi-agent nodes have a single, strictly typed source of truth for execution memory.

Acceptance Criteria:

Create a directory at ai_engine/schemas/ containing an empty __init__.py.

Inside that directory, create state.py and define a Pydantic V2 class named WorkflowState inheriting from BaseModel.

The schema must enforce strict data types for the following execution variables:

metric_id: str

risk_type: str

location: str

metric_details: Dict[str, Any]

user_commentary: str

guidelines: List[str]

system_prompt: str (Default: "")

ai_draft: str (Default: "")

validation_passed: bool (Default: False)

validation_errors: List[str] (Default: [])

Provide a quick usage example in the code or comments showing how this model cleanly exports back to a mutable runtime dictionary using .model_dump().

🎫 Story 2: Dynamic YAML Prompt Template Manager (prompts/)
Epic: Phase 1 - Foundations

Description:

As a developer, I want to create a file-based prompt template loading system using YAML so that we can isolate complex human-language engineering parameters from our core execution code.

Acceptance Criteria:

Create a directory structure at ai_engine/prompts/templates/.

Create an initial configuration file named market_risk.yaml using multi-line literal blocks (|) to store the baseline system instructions. Ensure it contains a token for string injection like {location}.

Implement PromptTemplateManager class inside ai_engine/prompts/manager.py.

Implement the method generate_system_prompt(self, risk_type: str, location: str) -> str.

The manager must use yaml.safe_load() to dynamically fetch the file matching the parsed lowercase risk_type (e.g., "Market Risk" resolves to market_risk.yaml) and perform standard string interpolation to inject the location.

Write a basic unit test ensuring that running this engine loader returns a cleanly formatted prompt string without throwing parsing errors.
